::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L06 Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Slide note: Source checks prevent weak evidence. Which speaker note best supports it? {
=A. Checking author, date, and purpose helps students decide whether evidence is reliable enough to use.#Correct. This explains the slide and connects it to the presentation purpose.
~B. Source checks prevent weak evidence because source checks prevent weak evidence.#Incorrect. This repeats the slide without explaining it.
~C. I will read the slide exactly as written.#Incorrect. Reading the slide does not add oral explanation.
~D. The slide is about something from school.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not explain the slide.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L06 Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which slide note is strongest for integrated communication? {
~A. A full paragraph of speaker notes#Incorrect. Slides should not carry the entire speech.
=B. Revision improves clarity and cohesion.#Correct. This note is concise and gives the audience a clear anchor.
~C. Things about writing and stuff#Incorrect. This is vague and not presentation-ready.
~D. I will explain this later maybe#Incorrect. This does not communicate a clear idea.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.03 U06 L06 Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.03
In this lesson, what does "integrated" mean? {
~A. unrelated and separate#Incorrect. Integrated means parts work together, not separately.
~B. decorative and colorful#Incorrect. Decoration is not the meaning of integrated.
=C. combined so the parts work together#Correct. Oral explanation and digital slides should work together.
~D. confusing and crowded#Incorrect. Integrated communication should be clear, not crowded.
}

::MLA.ENG1.L.01 U06 L06 Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Which speaker note uses standard English? {
~A. Slide notes helps audiences follow ideas.#Incorrect. The verb does not agree with the plural subject.
~B. Slide notes helping audiences follow ideas.#Incorrect. This is a fragment. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Slide note help audience follows ideas.#Incorrect. This has multiple agreement errors.
=D. Slide notes help audiences follow ideas.#Correct. This sentence uses standard English agreement.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.03 U06 L06 Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.03
What makes a collaborative digital presentation publication-ready? {
=A. Clear wording, consistent formatting, and audience-aware content#Correct. These qualities help a presentation be ready to share.
~B. Random formatting and unclear slide notes#Incorrect. These would weaken publication quality.
~C. Unchecked spelling and missing audience focus#Incorrect. Publication-ready work should be polished.
~D. Crowded slides with unrelated paragraphs#Incorrect. Crowded, unrelated slides are not publication-ready.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L06 Q06::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which speaker behavior best supports digital slides? {
~A. Read every slide word for word and add nothing.#Incorrect. Oral delivery should explain, not merely repeat.
=B. Explain the meaning of each concise slide note.#Correct. This helps the audience understand how the slide supports the message.
~C. Ignore the slides completely.#Incorrect. Integrated communication requires connection between slides and speaking.
~D. Talk about unrelated details while slides appear.#Incorrect. That would confuse the audience. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L06 Q07::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which digital slide is most effective? {
~A. A paragraph with six full sentences#Incorrect. This is likely too crowded for a slide.
~B. A vague label that says "stuff"#Incorrect. This does not guide the audience. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=C. A concise note: "Feedback reveals unclear claims."#Correct. This note is short and connected to a clear idea.
~D. An unrelated picture with no caption#Incorrect. This would not support coherent ideas.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.03 U06 L06 Q08::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.03
The phrase "publication-ready" most nearly means what? {
~A. impossible to revise#Incorrect. Publication-ready work usually becomes stronger through revision.
~B. unfinished and full of placeholders#Incorrect. Publication-ready work should not be unfinished.
~C. unrelated to any audience#Incorrect. Publication-ready work should be tailored to an audience.
=D. polished enough to share with the intended audience#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L06 Q09::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Slide note: "Check author, date, purpose." Which oral explanation is best? {
=A. These three checks help students decide whether a source is trustworthy and useful for research.#Correct. This explains the slide's meaning. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. I put three words on the slide.#Incorrect. This does not explain why the checks matter.
~C. The slide is short, so I do not need to say anything.#Incorrect. A short slide still needs oral explanation.
~D. Author, date, and purpose are words.#Incorrect. This does not support the presentation purpose.
}

::MLA.ENG1.L.01 U06 L06 Q10::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.03
Which slide note is conventionally correct? {
~A. Feedback improve writing clarity.#Incorrect. The verb does not agree with the subject.
=B. Feedback improves writing clarity.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Feedback improving writing clarity.#Incorrect. This is a fragment. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. Feedback improve writing's clarity's.#Incorrect. This contains agreement and apostrophe errors.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L06 Q11::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which pairing works best? {
~A. Slide: "Revision improves clarity." Speaker: "Lunch schedules are confusing."#Incorrect. The speaker note is unrelated. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Slide: "Revision improves clarity." Speaker: "Revision improves clarity."#Incorrect. This only repeats the slide. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=C. Slide: "Revision improves clarity." Speaker: "Revision helps readers follow ideas because writers can strengthen wording and transitions."#Correct. The speaker note explains the slide without simply repeating it.
~D. Slide: "Revision improves clarity." Speaker: "Look at this slide silently."#Incorrect. The speaker should explain the slide.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.03 U06 L06 Q12::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which word has the strongest positive connotation for a presentation product? {
~A. unfinished#Incorrect. This suggests the work is not ready.
~B. sloppy#Incorrect. This has a negative connotation. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. random#Incorrect. Random suggests lack of organization.
=D. polished#Correct. Polished suggests careful revision and presentation-ready quality.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.03 U06 L06 Q13::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.03
In collaborative publication work, why should students review formatting consistency? {
=A. Consistent formatting helps the final product look organized and audience-ready.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Consistency makes the content less clear.#Incorrect. Consistency usually supports clarity.
~C. Formatting should be random in published work.#Incorrect. Random formatting weakens polish. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. Formatting replaces the need for clear ideas.#Incorrect. Formatting supports, but does not replace, content.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L06 Q14::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Which speaker note best avoids simply reading the slide? {
~A. My slide says feedback reveals unclear claims.#Incorrect. This mostly repeats the slide. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=B. Feedback reveals unclear claims because readers can point out where the writer's idea needs more explanation.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Feedback reveals unclear claims, feedback reveals unclear claims.#Incorrect. This repeats the slide. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. I will not explain this slide.#Incorrect. The speaker should explain the slide.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L06 Q15::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.03
A slide says, "Source credibility." What is the best revision? {
~A. Source credibility and all the many things that could happen with sources#Incorrect. This is wordy and unclear. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Stuff about whether sources are things#Incorrect. This is vague and confusing. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=C. Check source credibility before using evidence.#Correct. This revision is clear, concise, and action-oriented.
~D. Credibility source maybe use#Incorrect. This wording is unclear and not publication-ready.
}

::MLA.ENG1.L.01 U06 L06 Q16::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which sentence should be revised before publication? {
~A. Speaker notes explain the slide in more detail.#Incorrect. This sentence is correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Clear slides help audiences follow the message.#Incorrect. This sentence is correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Publication-ready work uses standard English.#Incorrect. This sentence is correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=D. Slide notes helps the audience understand the focus.#Correct. The verb should be "help" because the subject "notes" is plural.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.03 U06 L06 Q17::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
In the sentence "The slide is crowded," what does "crowded" suggest? {
=A. The slide has too much information or too many elements.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. The slide is reliable.#Incorrect. Crowded does not mean reliable. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. The slide has a clear focus.#Incorrect. Crowded often suggests the opposite.
~D. The slide is empty.#Incorrect. Crowded means too full, not empty.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.03 U06 L06 Q18::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.03
Which final check best supports publication-ready collaborative work? {
~A. Add unrelated text from every group member.#Incorrect. Unrelated text weakens the final product.
=B. Confirm that slide titles, wording, and formatting are clear and consistent.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. Leave spelling unchecked.#Incorrect. Publication-ready work should be edited.
~D. Remove the audience focus.#Incorrect. Audience focus is necessary for publication quality.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L06 Q19::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Which statement best describes integration? {
~A. The slides should contain every spoken word.#Incorrect. That would overload slides and reduce oral value.
~B. The speaker should ignore the slides.#Incorrect. That would separate the parts. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=C. The speaker and slides should communicate connected parts of the same message.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. The speaker should change topics while slides stay the same.#Incorrect. That would confuse the audience. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L06 Q20::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.03
Which slide note is most concise? {
~A. Writing and revising and readers and all that stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Revision is something that can maybe help people who read things in some ways.#Incorrect. This is too wordy and vague. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. The thing about revision is that it is a thing.#Incorrect. This is unclear and circular. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=D. Revision helps readers.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.03 U06 L06 Q21::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
What is the denotative meaning of "audience" in this lesson? {
=A. the people who receive or view the communication#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. the title of a slide#Incorrect. A title is not the audience. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. the color of a presentation#Incorrect. Color is a design choice, not the audience.
~D. the file name of a document#Incorrect. A file name is not the audience. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L06 Q22::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Which speaker note best matches the slide note "Evidence supports perspective"? {
~A. Evidence supports perspective; evidence supports perspective.#Incorrect. This repeats without explaining. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
=B. Evidence supports the perspective by giving the audience a reason to trust the claim.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~C. This slide is blue and white.#Incorrect. This does not explain the idea. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~D. I am done with this slide.#Incorrect. This does not support understanding.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.03 U06 L06 Q23::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.03
Which collaborative action is most appropriate before sharing a final presentation? {
~A. Each group member checks only their own slide and ignores the whole presentation.#Incorrect. The final product needs whole-presentation consistency.
~B. The group adds unrelated content to make it longer.#Incorrect. Length does not improve publication quality.
=C. The group reviews the full presentation for audience fit, conventions, and consistent formatting.#Correct. This supports publication-ready quality.
~D. The group removes all speaker notes.#Incorrect. Speaker notes support oral communication.
}

::MLA.ENG1.L.01 U06 L06 Q24::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.L.01
Which revision fixes the convention error? Original: "The slides supports the speaker." {
~A. The slides supporting the speaker.#Incorrect. This is a fragment. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. The slide supports the speakers.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning and does not keep the plural slide idea.
~C. The slides supports speaker's.#Incorrect. This keeps the agreement error and adds an apostrophe problem.
=D. The slides support the speaker.#Correct. The plural subject "slides" needs the verb "support."
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.02 U06 L06 Q25::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.03
Which final product best integrates oral and digital communication? {
=A. Concise slides with speaker notes that explain and extend each slide#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. Crowded slides with no oral explanation#Incorrect. This overloads the audience and weakens delivery.
~C. Speaker notes that discuss unrelated topics#Incorrect. Speaker notes must connect to the slides.
~D. Slides with random images and no clear perspective#Incorrect. This lacks coherent digital communication.
}
